    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Thebes was the largest and leading polis of the Boeotian League and was often called &quot;Seven-Gated Thebes&quot;.</li><li>The Theban army trained and led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a> defeated the Spartans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE, beginning a short Theban hegemony.</li><li>After a revolt in 335 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> destroyed the city and sold the Thebans into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> re-established it in 315 BCE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When Alexander destroyed Thebes in 335 BCE, tradition holds that he spared only the temples and the house of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a>, along with priests, the pro-Macedonian party and Pindar's descendants.</p></aside>
    </section>

