    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cadmus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cadmus.jpg" alt="Cadmus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cadmus">Cadmus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kadmos_dragon_Louvre_N3157.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Python, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Excavations in and around Thebes have revealed a Mycenaean settlement, cist graves holding weapons and ivory, and clay tablets written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> script, which name the place as te-qa-i, "at Thebes". It was one of the first Greek communities drawn together within a fortified city, and it owed its importance to its military strength. In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> it drew resources from Lamos near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-helicon">Mount Helicon</a> and from Karystos and Amarynthos on Euboia.</p><p>The central area, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Cadmea</a>, shows signs of destruction towards the end of the Mycenaean era, and much of the site was abandoned. The Homeric Hypothebai, or "sub-Thebes", may have been the seed of the Archaic and Classical polity when the city was reestablished. Greek legend made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmus">Cadmus</a>, a Phoenician prince from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, the founder of the citadel and the teacher of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet</a>, and set at Thebes the stories of Semele and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>, Amphion's seven-gated wall, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oedipus">Oedipus</a> and the Seven against Thebes, and the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>. The Theban nobility bore the name Spartoi, "Sown Men", explained by the myth of Cadmus.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pindar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pindar.jpg" alt="Pindar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Pindar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NikonZ7II, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Thebes stands in a plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> between Lake Yliki, the ancient Hylica, to the north and the Cithaeron mountains, which divide Boeotia from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, to the south, about 50 km northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. The city was the largest in Boeotia and the centre of the region, and its rule over the smaller Boeotian towns was the constant aim of its policy and the constant cause of its quarrels with Athens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>.</p><p>The saved sources say little of the classical city's economy beyond its standing as the chief market and fortress of the plain. Its society was led for long periods by a traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>, the governing aristocracy that took the Persian side in 480 BCE, until a democratic constitution replaced it after 379 BCE. Thebes produced the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a>, the painters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristides">Aristides</a> and Nicomachus and the Cynic philosopher Crates. It was famous in antiquity for the practice of pedagogic pederasty, for which some ancients found an origin in the myth of Laius and Chrysippus. Thebes was revered as the most ancient of Greek cities, with a history of over 1,000 years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra.jpg" alt="Battle of Leuctra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Battle of Leuctra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pelopidas_leading_the_Thebans_at_the_battle_of_Leuctra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Edmund Ollier Publication date 1882, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Thebes rose on its army. In 506 BCE the Thebans repelled an inroad into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>; in 480 BCE they sent 400 men to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>, who were defeated alongside the Spartans, and then fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Battle of Plataea</a> in 479 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Cadmea</a> held out as a base when the Athenians occupied the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> from 457 to 447 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> the Thebans besieged Plataea in 431 BCE, destroyed it after its capture in 427 BCE and, in 424 BCE, defeated an Athenian army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-delium">Battle of Delium</a>.</p><p>They stood their ground against the Spartans at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-haliartus">Battle of Haliartus</a> in 395 BCE and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-coronea-394-bc">Battle of Coronea</a> in 394 BCE. Trained and led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a>, with the Sacred Band of Thebes, the Theban army won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE, then carried its arms into the Peloponnesus, freeing many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a>, and sent expeditions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>. Epaminondas fell at Mantinea in 362 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-sacred-war">Third Sacred War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a> (356–346 BCE) Thebes lost its predominance, and its contingent lost the decisive Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epaminondas.jpg" alt="Epaminondas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epaminondas_-_Temple_of_Ancient_Virtue,_Stowe_-_Buckinghamshire,_England_-_DSC07376_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daderot, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Thebes headed the Boeotian League, and its presidency of the league was the measure of its power: the victorious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> took it away after 479 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> restored Thebes as the dominant power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> in 457 BCE, and the general settlement of 387 BCE, which declared the autonomy of all Greek towns, withdrew the other Boeotians from its control again. Thebes was also a member of the Delphic amphictyony, from which Sparta tried and failed to expel it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Persian Wars</a>.</p><p>For much of its history the city was governed by a traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>. In 379 BCE, when the Spartan garrison that had held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Cadmea</a> since 382 BCE was expelled, a democratic constitution was set up in its place. The leading men of that democracy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a>, were generals and statesmen at once. After Chaeronea Philip II deprived Thebes of its dominion over Boeotia, and the destruction of 335 BCE ended the city's independent government until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> restored it. In its last free decades it changed masters often, recovering its autonomy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Demetrius Poliorcetes</a> in 287 BCE and allying itself with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Philip II of Macedon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phillip_II,_king_of_Macedonia,_Roman_copy_of_Greek_original,_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek,_Copenhagen_(36420294055).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Mortel from Riyadh, Saudi Arabia, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Thebes changed the balance of the Greek world more than once. Its aristocracy's support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> in 480–479 BCE marked it for generations, and its hostility to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> made it a firm ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>. After Athens fell, Thebes turned on Sparta, and the victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE permanently crippled Spartan power, in part by freeing many of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a> on whom the Spartan economy rested. Its army was regulating the affairs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> in the following years.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> had been raised in Thebes as a hostage and had learnt much of the art of war from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a>, so the city also shaped the power that overthrew it. Its destruction in 335 BCE cowed Athens into submission. Ancient writers treated the destruction as excessive, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> reports that Alexander later tried to make amends to any Theban he met. Beyond politics, Thebes gave Greek literature the great Theban cycle of legend, the stories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmus">Cadmus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oedipus">Oedipus</a>, the Seven against Thebes and the Epigoni, which rival the myths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> in their influence on classical writing.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/great-hymn-to-the-aten">Great Hymn to the Aten<span>c. 1350 BCE</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/demosthenes.jpg" alt="Demosthenes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Demosthenes_Polyeuctos_Louvre_Ma237_n01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Polyeuctus, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Theban hegemony was short-lived: the states it protected would not accept its control for long, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> worked to prevent a Theban empire. With the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> at Mantinea in 362 BCE Thebes became a secondary power. By asking Philip II to crush the Phocians it brought Macedonian power close to its borders, and in 338 BCE, persuaded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a>, it joined Athens and lost at Chaeronea. When it revolted against Alexander in 335 BCE while he campaigned in the north, he and his Greek allies destroyed the city, divided its land among the other Boeotian cities and sold its people into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> re-established Thebes in 315 BCE; the Athenians rebuilt much of its wall, and help came from Megalopolis, Messene, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The city never regained its former standing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Demetrius Poliorcetes</a> besieged it in 293 BCE and again after a revolt in 292 BCE, taking it a second time. After the dissolution of the Boeotian League following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaean-war">Achaean War</a> of 146 BCE, Thebes came under Roman rule, and in 27 BCE it was included in the Province of Achaia. The city itself lived on, later the capital of the Byzantine Theme of Hellas and a great centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> weaving.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Thebes was the chief city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> and head of the Boeotian League. It sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 480 BCE, fought beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and then against it, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> won the hegemony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE. Defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> at Chaeronea and destroyed by Alexander in 335 BCE, it was rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> but never recovered, and came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 146 BCE.</em></p>

